// Major Prophets: context every 4–5 chapters plus Bible-college study questions.
// s/e = start/end chapter. qs = study questions with "prep" = points a strong answer should cover.
window.SECTIONS = [
// ================= ISAIAH =================
{b:"Isaiah",s:1,e:5,title:"A sick nation and a vineyard gone wild",
ctx:"c. 740 BC. Judah is wealthy under Uzziah and Jotham, but its worship is hollow: sacrifices with no justice. Isaiah opens like a courtroom: heaven and earth are called as witnesses against God's children. Chapter 2 sees a future when the nations stream to Zion. Chapter 5's Song of the Vineyard shows God's people producing 'bloodshed' instead of justice.",
themes:["Covenant lawsuit","Empty ritual vs. justice","Zion's future glory","Six woes"],
qs:[{q:"Why does God reject Judah's sacrifices in Isaiah 1:10-17 when He Himself commanded them?",prep:"Worship cut off from obedience and justice; 1 Samuel 15:22; Hosea 6:6. God's remedy in 1:16-18: 'wash, learn to do good.' The problem is the heart, not the ritual."},
{q:"Compare the vineyard song (Isaiah 5:1-7) with Jesus' parable of the tenants (Matthew 21:33-44).",prep:"Same imagery of God planting and caring for a vineyard. Isaiah judges fruitlessness; Jesus adds the killed Son. Wordplay in 5:7: mishpat/mishpach (justice/bloodshed), tsedaqah/tse'aqah (righteousness/a cry)."}]},
{b:"Isaiah",s:6,e:9,title:"The call and the sign of Immanuel",
ctx:"Isaiah 6 happens 'in the year King Uzziah died' (c. 740 BC). Isaiah sees the true King, holy, holy, holy. Chapters 7–8 take place during the Syro-Ephraimite crisis (c. 734 BC): Aram and Israel threaten Jerusalem to force it into an anti-Assyria alliance. King Ahaz refuses to trust God and hires Assyria instead. God gives the sign of Immanuel and promises a Child who will sit on David's throne (9:6-7).",
themes:["God's holiness","Faith vs. political alliances","Immanuel","Light to Galilee"],
qs:[{q:"Explain the commission in Isaiah 6:9-10 to 'make the heart of this people dull.' How does the NT use it?",prep:"Judicial hardening: preaching confirms a heart already set. Quoted in Matthew 13:14-15, John 12:40 and Acts 28:26-27. God's sovereignty and human responsibility. A remnant 'holy seed' remains (6:13)."},
{q:"How is Isaiah 7:14 both a sign to Ahaz and a prophecy of Christ?",prep:"Near sign: within a few years (7:15-16) the threat from the two kings would end. 'Almah' means a young woman of marriageable age; the Septuagint has 'parthenos' (virgin). Matthew 1:23 shows the full fulfilment. The Child of 9:6 is called 'Mighty God,' which only Jesus fulfils."}]},
{b:"Isaiah",s:10,e:12,title:"Assyria the rod, and the Shoot from Jesse",
ctx:"Assyria (under Tiglath-Pileser III and later Sennacherib) is God's 'rod of anger' against a godless nation, but Assyria is arrogant and will be judged too (10:5-19). From the stump of David's family a Shoot will rise, filled with the sevenfold Spirit, bringing a peaceable kingdom where the wolf lives with the lamb. Chapter 12 is a song of salvation.",
themes:["God uses and judges nations","The Messiah's Spirit-anointing","Remnant returns","Song of praise"],
qs:[{q:"How can God use Assyria as His instrument yet hold Assyria guilty (10:5-15)?",prep:"Same act, different intentions: God's purpose is discipline, Assyria's is plunder and pride. Compare Genesis 50:20 and Acts 2:23. The axe cannot boast over the one who swings it."},
{q:"What does Isaiah 11:1-10 teach about the Messiah's character and kingdom?",prep:"Root of Jesse = David's line brought low. The Spirit rests on Him (compare His baptism). He judges the poor justly. The new creation of peace, with the earth full of the knowledge of the LORD. Paul applies 11:10 to Gentiles in Romans 15:12."}]},
{b:"Isaiah",s:13,e:17,title:"Oracles against the nations, part 1",
ctx:"Isaiah turns to the nations around Judah: Babylon (13–14), Philistia (14:28-32), Moab (15–16) and Damascus and Ephraim (17). Babylon then was a rising rebel city inside Assyria's empire, and it later becomes the symbol of human pride. The taunt in 14:12-15 against the 'Day Star, son of the dawn' is aimed at Babylon's king.",
themes:["God rules all nations","Pride brought low","Day of the LORD","Grief for Moab"],
qs:[{q:"Discuss interpretations of Isaiah 14:12-15. Is it about Satan, the king of Babylon, or both?",prep:"Context: a taunt against the king of Babylon (14:4). The language goes beyond a human king (heaven, throne above the stars). Many interpreters see a type pointing to Satan's pride, compare Luke 10:18. Avoid overstating it; make the primary meaning clear first."},
{q:"Why would Judah need oracles about foreign nations?",prep:"Do not trust or fear these nations. God is sovereign over all of history. Even enemies' downfall is in His hands. There is hope for nations too (19:24-25)."}]},
{b:"Isaiah",s:18,e:23,title:"Oracles against the nations, part 2",
ctx:"Oracles continue against Cush, Egypt, Babylon, Edom (Dumah), Arabia, Jerusalem itself (22, the 'Valley of Vision') and Tyre (23). In chapter 20 Isaiah walks barefoot and stripped for three years as a sign that Egypt and Cush, whom Judah trusted, would be led captive by Assyria (Ashdod rebellion, 711 BC). The surprise of 19:23-25: Egypt and Assyria will one day worship alongside Israel.",
themes:["Don't trust Egypt","Prophetic sign-acts","Jerusalem's false security","Mission to nations"],
qs:[{q:"What is shocking about Isaiah 19:24-25, and what does it anticipate?",prep:"Israel's worst enemies called 'My people' and 'the work of My hands.' This anticipates Gentile inclusion (Genesis 12:3, Ephesians 2:11-22)."},
{q:"What is the sin of Jerusalem in Isaiah 22:8-14?",prep:"They prepared their defenses (water, walls, weapons) 'but you did not look to Him who did it.' Partying instead of repenting: 'let us eat and drink' (quoted in 1 Corinthians 15:32). Shebna versus Eliakim, and the key of David (Revelation 3:7)."}]},
{b:"Isaiah",s:24,e:27,title:"The 'Little Apocalypse'",
ctx:"Isaiah zooms out from individual nations to the whole earth: universal judgment, then a great feast on Mount Zion where God swallows up death forever (25:6-8). Resurrection hope appears (26:19). Leviathan the serpent is slain (27:1). These chapters look ahead to the end of history and are not tied to one dated event.",
themes:["Cosmic judgment","Messianic banquet","Death swallowed up","Resurrection"],
qs:[{q:"Trace Isaiah 25:8 into the New Testament.",prep:"1 Corinthians 15:54: 'Death is swallowed up in victory.' Revelation 7:17 and 21:4: God wipes away tears. The banquet links to the marriage supper of the Lamb (Revelation 19) and Jesus' table (Matthew 8:11)."},
{q:"How does Isaiah 26:3-4 speak to anxiety in the context of judgment?",prep:"'Perfect peace' (shalom shalom) for the mind stayed on God. Trust rests on God as the 'everlasting Rock,' not on circumstances. Compare Philippians 4:6-7."}]},
{b:"Isaiah",s:28,e:31,title:"Woes on Ephraim and Jerusalem: don't run to Egypt",
ctx:"c. 705–701 BC. After Sargon II dies, Hezekiah's advisors plot rebellion against Assyria and send envoys to Egypt for help. Isaiah calls it a 'covenant with death.' God has laid a tested cornerstone in Zion (28:16). The repeated message: 'In returning and rest you shall be saved; in quietness and trust shall be your strength' (30:15).",
themes:["Political panic vs. faith","The cornerstone","Lip-service worship","Waiting on God"],
qs:[{q:"How does the NT use the 'cornerstone' of Isaiah 28:16?",prep:"Romans 9:33 and 10:11, 1 Peter 2:6: Christ is the stone. The one who believes will not be put to shame. He is a stumbling stone for those who reject Him. Also Psalm 118:22."},
{q:"Jesus quoted Isaiah 29:13. What does it expose about religion?",prep:"Mark 7:6-7. Honoring with lips while the heart is far away. Man-made rules replacing God's word. Apply it to modern church culture."}]},
{b:"Isaiah",s:32,e:35,title:"The righteous King and the desert in bloom",
ctx:"Woes give way to hope: a King who reigns in righteousness (32), God rising against the destroyer Assyria (33), judgment on Edom as a symbol of hostile nations (34), and the glorious return in which the desert blossoms and the ransomed of the LORD return to Zion with singing (35). Chapter 35 previews themes developed in chapters 40–66.",
themes:["Righteous reign","Outpouring of the Spirit","Reversal","The Highway of Holiness"],
qs:[{q:"Jesus answered John the Baptist with language from Isaiah 35:5-6. Why?",prep:"Matthew 11:4-5: the blind see, the lame walk. Jesus is saying the promised restoration has begun in Him. Signs point to the new creation."},
{q:"What does Isaiah 32:15-17 teach about the Spirit and righteousness?",prep:"Desolation lasts 'until the Spirit is poured upon us from on high.' Then the wilderness becomes fruitful, and the effect of righteousness is peace. Compare Joel 2:28 and Acts 2."}]},
{b:"Isaiah",s:36,e:39,title:"Hezekiah: Assyria at the gates (the historical bridge)",
ctx:"701 BC. Sennacherib of Assyria has captured 46 Judean cities (also recorded in his own annals) and surrounds Jerusalem. The Rabshakeh mocks trust in the LORD. Hezekiah spreads the letter before God, Isaiah prophesies deliverance, and the angel of the LORD strikes 185,000 Assyrian soldiers. Then Hezekiah is healed and given 15 more years, but he shows Babylon's envoys his treasures. Isaiah predicts the Babylonian exile, which sets up chapters 40–66.",
themes:["Prayer in crisis","God defends His city","Pride before Babylon","A bridge from Assyria to Babylon"],
qs:[{q:"Compare Ahaz in Isaiah 7 with Hezekiah in Isaiah 37. What is Isaiah teaching?",prep:"Both kings face invasion, and Isaiah's message is the same: trust. Ahaz trusts Assyria; Hezekiah prays. Faith versus fear decides each king's story. Compare their locations (7:3 and 36:2, the same aqueduct)."},
{q:"Why does Isaiah 39 matter for the structure of the book?",prep:"It shifts the focus from Assyria to Babylon. The prediction of exile leads straight into 40:1 'Comfort, comfort My people.' It shows that even good kings fail, so a better King is needed."}]},
{b:"Isaiah",s:40,e:44,title:"Comfort, My people: the incomparable God",
ctx:"The setting moves ahead to the exiles in Babylon (c. 550–539 BC), though conservative scholars hold that Isaiah wrote it prophetically. Their sin is pardoned, a voice cries 'Prepare the way.' God is incomparable to idols, which are mocked as wood that one half cooks dinner and the other half is worshiped (44). The first 'Servant Song' appears (42:1-9), and Cyrus is named in advance as the deliverer (44:28).",
themes:["Comfort","No god like Yahweh","Idolatry satirized","The Servant introduced","Cyrus named"],
qs:[{q:"How does Isaiah 40 frame John the Baptist and the gospel?",prep:"40:3 is quoted for John in all four Gospels. 'Here is your God!' (40:9) as good news. The Shepherd-King (40:11; John 10). God gives power to the weary (40:29-31)."},
{q:"What is the argument of the idol satire in Isaiah 44:9-20?",prep:"Idols are made by people, need carrying, and cannot speak, save or predict the future. In contrast, God predicts Cyrus by name. Modern idols are also 'a lie in my right hand' (44:20)."}]},
{b:"Isaiah",s:45,e:48,title:"Cyrus, God's anointed, and the fall of Babylon",
ctx:"God calls Cyrus, the Persian who would conquer Babylon in 539 BC, His 'anointed' to free the exiles, though Cyrus does not know God (45:1-4). Babylon's gods Bel and Nebo must be carried as burdens, while God carries His people from birth to old age (46). Proud 'Lady Babylon' falls (47). Chapter 48 rebukes stubborn Israel and calls: 'Go out from Babylon!'",
themes:["Sovereignty over history","God carries His people","The fall of Babylon","Leave Babylon"],
qs:[{q:"Isaiah 45:7 says God 'creates calamity' (or 'evil'). How should we interpret this?",prep:"Hebrew 'ra' here means disaster, not moral evil (contrast 'shalom'). God is sovereign over light and dark, peace and calamity, but is never the author of sin (James 1:13). Context: Cyrus's conquests."},
{q:"How is Isaiah 45:22-23 applied to Jesus in Philippians 2:10-11?",prep:"'Every knee shall bow' to Yahweh in Isaiah; Paul applies it to Jesus. This is strong evidence of Christ's deity. Note the global invitation: 'Turn to Me and be saved, all the ends of the earth.'"}]},
{b:"Isaiah",s:49,e:53,title:"The Suffering Servant",
ctx:"Servant Songs 2–4 (49:1-6; 50:4-9; 52:13–53:12). The Servant is called 'Israel' (49:3) yet restores Israel (49:5), so He is an individual who embodies the nation. He is a light to the nations, struck and spat upon but resolute, and finally He bears the sins of many, is 'cut off,' and yet sees His offspring. These are among the clearest prophecies of Christ's death and resurrection in the Old Testament.",
themes:["Servant as true Israel","Light to the nations","Substitution","Exaltation through suffering"],
qs:[{q:"Exegete Isaiah 53:4-6. Identify the language of substitution.",prep:"'Our griefs... our sorrows... our transgressions... our iniquities.' He was pierced 'for' us. The LORD laid on Him the iniquity of us all. Compare 1 Peter 2:24-25 and Acts 8:32-35 (the Ethiopian). Answer the interpretation that the Servant is the nation: the Servant is innocent (53:9) and suffers for 'my people' (53:8)."},
{q:"How do Isaiah 52:13-15 and 53:10-12 point to the resurrection?",prep:"He is 'high and lifted up' (the same phrase as God in 6:1). After death 'He shall see His offspring, prolong His days.' He divides the spoil with the strong. He makes intercession (Hebrews 7:25)."}]},
{b:"Isaiah",s:54,e:57,title:"The great invitation",
ctx:"Because of the Servant's work, barren Zion is told to sing (54), and an open invitation is issued: 'Come, everyone who thirsts... without money' (55). God's word will not return empty. Foreigners and eunuchs who hold fast to the covenant are welcomed (56). Chapter 57 contrasts corrupt idolatrous leaders with God, who dwells with the contrite and lowly.",
themes:["Grace invitation","The power of God's word","Inclusion of outsiders","God near the humble"],
qs:[{q:"What does Isaiah 55:8-11 teach about Scripture's power?",prep:"God's thoughts are higher. His word is like rain: it accomplishes His purpose. This gives confidence for preaching and Bible reading. The context is the invitation to seek the LORD (55:6-7)."},
{q:"How does Isaiah 56:3-8 anticipate Acts 8 and Mark 11:17?",prep:"Eunuchs and foreigners are welcomed. The Ethiopian eunuch reads Isaiah 53. Jesus quotes 'house of prayer for all nations' when He clears the temple."}]},
{b:"Isaiah",s:58,e:62,title:"True fasting and the Anointed Preacher",
ctx:"Back in the land (likely addressing the post-exilic community, compare Ezra and Nehemiah), religion is again hollow. God defines true fasting as loosing injustice and feeding the hungry (58). Sin separates people from God (59), but the Redeemer comes to Zion. Zion's glory and the 'Spirit of the Lord is upon Me' passage, which Jesus read in Nazareth (61:1-2; Luke 4:18-19), follow.",
themes:["True vs. false fasting","Sin separates","The Redeemer","Jubilee and good news"],
qs:[{q:"Why did Jesus stop reading in the middle of Isaiah 61:2 at Nazareth?",prep:"He read 'the year of the LORD's favor' but not 'the day of vengeance.' His first coming brings grace; judgment comes at His second coming. This is the 'already/not yet' of the kingdom."},
{q:"Summarize God's definition of fasting in Isaiah 58 and apply it.",prep:"Fasting while exploiting workers and fighting is rejected. True fasting means justice, feeding the hungry, housing the poor and not hiding from your own family. Promises of light and guidance follow (58:8-12). Sabbath delight (58:13-14)."}]},
{b:"Isaiah",s:63,e:66,title:"The Divine Warrior and new heavens and new earth",
ctx:"The book ends with the Warrior returning from Edom with stained garments (63, compare Revelation 19:13), a heartfelt prayer of lament ('Oh that You would rend the heavens,' 64), and God's answer: new heavens and a new earth (65:17), worship from all nations, and judgment on the rebels (66). The book that began with heaven and earth as witnesses (1:2) ends with a new heaven and earth.",
themes:["Divine Warrior","Lament prayer","New creation","Mission to all nations","Final judgment"],
qs:[{q:"How does Revelation 21–22 build on Isaiah 65–66?",prep:"New heavens and earth, no more weeping, the new Jerusalem, and the nations bringing glory. Isaiah describes it with long lifespans in poetic terms; Revelation adds 'no more death.' Ultimate fulfilment is in Christ."},
{q:"Why does Isaiah end on such a sober note (66:24), and how did Jesus use it?",prep:"There are two destinies. Mark 9:48: 'their worm does not die.' Hope and warning belong together. A pastoral, honest presentation of judgment."}]},
// ================= JEREMIAH =================
{b:"Jeremiah",s:1,e:4,title:"The call and the broken cisterns",
ctx:"627 BC, the 13th year of King Josiah. Jeremiah, a young priest from Anathoth, is told 'Before I formed you in the womb I knew you.' Visions of an almond branch (God is watching) and a boiling pot from the north (Babylon) set the tone. Even during Josiah's reforms, Judah's heart is still unfaithful: they have forsaken the fountain of living water for broken cisterns (2:13).",
themes:["Called before birth","God watching over His word","Spiritual adultery","Circumcise your hearts"],
qs:[{q:"What does Jeremiah 1:4-10 teach about calling and inadequacy?",prep:"God's foreknowledge and appointment. 'I am only a youth.' God answers with His presence ('I am with you') and His words placed in Jeremiah's mouth. Compare Moses (Exodus 4) and Paul (Galatians 1:15)."},
{q:"Unpack the two evils of Jeremiah 2:13.",prep:"Forsaking God AND making substitutes. Living water versus cracked, plastered pits. Compare John 4:10-14 and 7:37-38. Identify modern 'cisterns.'"}]},
{b:"Jeremiah",s:5,e:8,title:"The temple sermon: no one is just",
ctx:"Jeremiah searches Jerusalem for one just person (5:1). In the famous Temple Sermon (7, likely 609 BC at the start of Jehoiakim's reign; compare 26) he warns people chanting 'The temple of the LORD!' that the building will not save them. Shiloh was destroyed, and so could this house be. False prophets heal the wound lightly: 'Peace, peace,' when there is no peace (6:14; 8:11).",
themes:["False security","Den of robbers","False prophets","Is there no balm?"],
qs:[{q:"How did Jesus use Jeremiah 7:11, and why is it significant?",prep:"Matthew 21:13, 'den of robbers,' when He cleared the temple. A robber's den is a hideout, not where robbing takes place. The people sinned, then hid in religion. This implies judgment on Herod's temple (destroyed AD 70)."},
{q:"What does 'Peace, peace, when there is no peace' (6:14) warn us about in ministry?",prep:"Shallow comfort without repentance. Leaders who tell people what they want to hear. Compare 2 Timothy 4:3. Real healing requires an honest diagnosis."}]},
{b:"Jeremiah",s:9,e:12,title:"The weeping prophet and the ruined belt",
ctx:"Jeremiah longs for his head to be a fountain of tears (9:1). Do not boast in wisdom, might or riches, but in knowing God (9:23-24). He exposes idols as scarecrows in a cucumber field (10:5). Covenant curses are announced (11), and his own family at Anathoth plots to kill him (11:18-23). Jeremiah complains: 'Why does the way of the wicked prosper?' (12:1).",
themes:["Grief for sin","Boast in the LORD","The living God vs. idols","Honest complaint"],
qs:[{q:"Jeremiah 9:23-24 is quoted by Paul. Where, and why?",prep:"1 Corinthians 1:31 and 2 Corinthians 10:17. Against pride in wisdom or status. What God delights in: steadfast love, justice and righteousness."},
{q:"What can we learn from Jeremiah's complaints (12:1-4) about prayer?",prep:"He is honest but still addresses God ('Righteous are You'). God's answer (12:5): 'if you've raced men, how will you run with horses?' God prepares him for harder days rather than explaining. Compare Habakkuk."}]},
{b:"Jeremiah",s:13,e:16,title:"Sign-acts and a forbidden marriage",
ctx:"Jeremiah buries a linen belt by the Euphrates (or Perath) until it rots, a picture of Judah's pride rotting (13). Drought strikes (14). God tells Jeremiah not to pray for the people (14:11) and that even Moses and Samuel could not turn His judgment (15:1). Jeremiah is forbidden to marry or attend funerals and feasts, and his life becomes a living sermon of coming loss (16).",
themes:["Prophetic sign-acts","Judgment that cannot be reversed","Prophet's loneliness","A new exodus promised"],
qs:[{q:"What does it mean when God says 'Do not pray for this people' (14:11)?",prep:"Judgment was fixed after persistent rebellion. It is not a general rule against prayer. Compare 1 John 5:16. Jeremiah kept interceding anyway in 14:19-22. God's patience does have an end."},
{q:"Why was Jeremiah's singleness (16:1-9) a prophetic message?",prep:"Children born would die in the siege. His life embodied the coming grief. Compare Hosea's and Ezekiel's lives. Obedience can cost personal dreams. A new exodus hope follows (16:14-15)."}]},
{b:"Jeremiah",s:17,e:20,title:"The deceitful heart and the potter's house",
ctx:"Judah's sin is engraved with an iron pen on the heart (17:1). Cursed is the one who trusts in man; blessed is the one who trusts in the LORD, like a tree by water (17:5-8). The heart is deceitful above all things (17:9). At the potter's house God shows He can reshape a nation (18). Jeremiah smashes a clay jar in the Valley of Hinnom (19), is beaten and put in stocks by Pashhur, and pours out his deepest lament: fire shut up in my bones (20:9).",
themes:["The corrupt heart","Trust vs. self-reliance","God's sovereignty as Potter","Persecution and lament"],
qs:[{q:"How does the potter image (18:1-11) balance God's sovereignty and human response?",prep:"God has the right to reshape. Yet 18:7-10 says that if a nation repents, God relents. His warnings are conditional. Paul uses the potter in Romans 9:20-21."},
{q:"Discuss Jeremiah 20:7-18 as a model of lament in ministry.",prep:"He feels deceived and mocked. Fire in his bones: he cannot stop speaking. He praises (20:13) then curses his birthday (20:14). Faith and despair sit side by side. God tolerates honesty."}]},
{b:"Jeremiah",s:21,e:24,title:"Shepherds, kings and the Righteous Branch",
ctx:"Zedekiah asks Jeremiah if God will save Jerusalem from Nebuchadnezzar (588 BC). The answer: surrender to Babylon and live. Messages to the last kings follow: Shallum, Jehoiakim and Coniah, of whom it is said none of his offspring shall sit on David's throne (22:30). Woe to the shepherds, but God will raise a Righteous Branch, 'The LORD Our Righteousness' (23:5-6). Two baskets of figs: the exiles in Babylon are the good figs (24).",
themes:["Failed shepherds","The Righteous Branch","False prophets' dreams","Good figs in exile"],
qs:[{q:"How does the curse on Coniah (22:30) relate to Jesus' genealogy?",prep:"Coniah (Jeconiah) is in Joseph's line (Matthew 1:11-12). Jesus is Joseph's legal heir but not his biological son, being born of a virgin. Luke's genealogy runs through Nathan. This is a common Bible-college discussion point, so present it carefully as one proposed solution."},
{q:"What marks a false prophet in Jeremiah 23:9-40?",prep:"They speak visions of their own minds. They promise peace to the stubborn. They steal words from one another. Compare God's word as fire and hammer (23:29). They did not stand in God's council. Apply it: test teaching by Scripture."}]},
{b:"Jeremiah",s:25,e:29,title:"Seventy years and the letter to the exiles",
ctx:"605 BC, the year Nebuchadnezzar rises: Jeremiah announces 70 years of Babylonian rule (25:11-12), which Daniel later reads (Daniel 9:2). The temple sermon nearly costs him his life (26). He wears a yoke to urge submission to Babylon (27) and confronts the false prophet Hananiah, who dies within the year (28). His letter tells the exiles to build houses, seek the city's welfare, and wait for God's plans (29:11).",
themes:["The 70 years","Submit to Babylon","True vs. false prophets","Thriving in exile"],
qs:[{q:"Interpret Jeremiah 29:11 in context. How should Christians apply it?",prep:"Spoken to exiles facing 70 years, most of whom would die before the return. It is a promise to the community about the long view, not instant personal success. Context: 29:4-7 (seek the city's peace) and 29:12-13 (seek God wholeheartedly). It does apply to God's faithful purposes for His people, Romans 8:28."},
{q:"How should we discern between Jeremiah and Hananiah (Jeremiah 28)?",prep:"Hananiah predicted a return within 2 years. Deuteronomy 18:22 gives the test of fulfilment. Jeremiah: the prophet of peace is proven when his word comes true. Jeremiah's humble 'Amen, may the LORD do so.' Hananiah's death."}]},
{b:"Jeremiah",s:30,e:33,title:"The Book of Comfort: the New Covenant",
ctx:"In the darkest days, during the siege of 588–587 BC, with Jeremiah imprisoned, God tells him to write down hope. Rachel weeping for her children (31:15) gives way to restoration. The New Covenant (31:31-34) promises a law written on hearts, personal knowledge of God, and sins remembered no more. Jeremiah buys a field at Anathoth while Babylon surrounds the city, a faith investment (32). David's line and the priesthood will endure (33).",
themes:["Hope in judgment","The New Covenant","Faith in action","Call to Me and I will answer"],
qs:[{q:"Explain the New Covenant of Jeremiah 31:31-34 and how Hebrews 8 applies it.",prep:"New versus the Sinai covenant they broke. Internal law, the heart, relationship and forgiveness. Hebrews 8:8-12 is the longest OT quotation in the NT. Jesus at the Last Supper: 'the new covenant in My blood' (Luke 22:20). Address Israel's future as well (Romans 11:27)."},
{q:"Why did God have Jeremiah buy a field during the siege (Jeremiah 32)?",prep:"A prophetic act of hope: 'Houses and fields and vineyards shall again be bought.' The kinsman's right of redemption. Jeremiah's prayer: 'Nothing is too hard for You' (32:17, 27). Obedience before understanding."}]},
{b:"Jeremiah",s:34,e:36,title:"Broken promises and the burned scroll",
ctx:"During the siege the people free their Hebrew slaves, then take them back when Babylon briefly withdraws (34), breaking a covenant made 'cutting the calf in two.' The Rechabites, who obey their ancestor's command not to drink wine, shame Judah, which will not obey God (35). In 605–604 BC King Jehoiakim cuts up Jeremiah's scroll and burns it, and God has it rewritten with more (36).",
themes:["Covenant faithfulness","Obedience shames disobedience","God's word can't be destroyed"],
qs:[{q:"What does the burning of the scroll (36) teach about God's word?",prep:"The king cuts and burns it without fear, while his father Josiah tore his robes on hearing the Law (2 Kings 22). The word is rewritten with more added. Compare Isaiah 40:8 and Matthew 24:35. Different responses to Scripture."},
{q:"Why does God use the Rechabites as an example (35)?",prep:"They obeyed a human ancestor for 200+ years; Judah would not obey God. It's about faithfulness, not about wine as such. God's promise to them in 35:19."}]},
{b:"Jeremiah",s:37,e:40,title:"The cistern and the fall of Jerusalem",
ctx:"Jeremiah is arrested as a deserter and thrown into a muddy cistern (38). He is rescued by Ebed-Melech, a Cushite eunuch. Zedekiah secretly consults him but is too afraid to obey. In July 586 BC the wall is breached: Zedekiah flees, his sons are killed before his eyes, and he is blinded and taken to Babylon (39). The Babylonians free Jeremiah, and Gedaliah becomes governor (40).",
themes:["Faithfulness under persecution","Fear of man","Judgment fulfilled","God remembers the faithful (Ebed-Melech)"],
qs:[{q:"Contrast Zedekiah and Ebed-Melech.",prep:"The king knows the truth but fears people. A foreign servant risks his life for the prophet and receives a promise of life 'because you trusted in Me' (39:18). Faith is not about status."},
{q:"How does Jerusalem's fall vindicate Jeremiah's 40-year ministry?",prep:"Every detail came true. Rejected for decades, yet faithful. Faithfulness, not apparent success, is the measure of a prophet. Compare 2 Timothy 4:2-5."}]},
{b:"Jeremiah",s:41,e:45,title:"Running to Egypt",
ctx:"Ishmael murders Gedaliah (41). Terrified of Babylonian revenge, the remnant asks Jeremiah to seek God, promising to obey. God says 'Stay in the land, do not go to Egypt.' They refuse, go to Egypt, and drag Jeremiah with them (42–43). In Egypt the women insist on worshiping the 'queen of heaven' (44). A short personal word to Baruch closes the section: 'Do you seek great things for yourself? Seek them not' (45:5).",
themes:["Asking for guidance you won't follow","Return to Egypt","Persistent idolatry","Ambition surrendered"],
qs:[{q:"What warning does Jeremiah 42–43 give about seeking God's will?",prep:"They asked for guidance having already decided. They called the true word a lie (43:2). Fear drives disobedience. James 1:22."},
{q:"What is God saying to Baruch in chapter 45, and why is it placed here?",prep:"Baruch was disappointed and ambitious. God promises him his life as a 'prize of war.' Contentment amid national collapse. It's placed after the narrative and before the nations oracles, dated 605 BC."}]},
{b:"Jeremiah",s:46,e:49,title:"Oracles to the nations",
ctx:"Jeremiah was appointed 'a prophet to the nations' (1:5). Egypt is defeated at Carchemish (605 BC, 46). Philistia (47), Moab (48), Ammon, Edom, Damascus, Kedar and Elam (49) follow. God's sovereignty extends to every nation, and surprising notes of restoration are promised to Egypt, Moab, Ammon and Elam.",
themes:["God judges every nation","The battle of Carchemish","Pride of Moab and Edom","Mercy even for enemies"],
qs:[{q:"Why do several oracles end with 'I will restore the fortunes' (48:47; 49:6, 39)?",prep:"God's mercy reaches beyond Israel. This anticipates Gentile salvation. Judgment is not God's last word for nations that turn."},
{q:"What is the historical significance of Carchemish (46:2)?",prep:"605 BC: Nebuchadnezzar defeats Pharaoh Necho and Babylon becomes the superpower. The same year as Daniel's deportation (Daniel 1:1) and Jeremiah 25 and 36."}]},
{b:"Jeremiah",s:50,e:52,title:"Babylon's fall and the historical appendix",
ctx:"The hammer of the whole earth will itself be broken. Babylon will fall to a nation from the north (the Medes and Persians, 539 BC). Jeremiah sends a scroll to Babylon with Seraiah to be read and sunk in the Euphrates (51:59-64). Chapter 52, which parallels 2 Kings 24–25, retells Jerusalem's fall, ending with a glimmer of grace: exiled King Jehoiachin released and fed at the king's table (c. 561 BC).",
themes:["Babylon judged","Flee from Babylon","Fulfilment recorded","Grace in exile"],
qs:[{q:"How does Revelation 17–18 echo Jeremiah 50–51?",prep:"'Come out of her, My people' (51:45; Revelation 18:4). Sinking a stone (51:63; Revelation 18:21). Babylon as a symbol of the proud world-system."},
{q:"Why might the book end with Jehoiachin's release (52:31-34)?",prep:"David's line is alive. A sign that God keeps His promises (33:17) despite judgment. Hope for the exiles, pointing toward the Messiah."}]},
// ================= LAMENTATIONS =================
{b:"Lamentations",s:1,e:2,title:"How lonely sits the city",
ctx:"Shortly after August 586 BC. Jerusalem lies in ruins: the temple burned, people starving, and children dying in the streets. Chapter 1 personifies Zion as a widow with no one to comfort her. Chapter 2 admits the hardest truth: the LORD has become like an enemy, because of sin, as the prophets warned. Chapters 1–4 are acrostics, an ordered A-to-Z way of expressing grief.",
themes:["Grief made honest","Sin's consequences","God as Judge","No comforter"],
qs:[{q:"Why is it significant that Lamentations is written as acrostics?",prep:"Chapters 1, 2 and 4 have 22 verses following the Hebrew alphabet; chapter 3 has 66 verses, 3 for each letter. Grief is expressed 'from A to Z' and given structure. Chapter 5 is not an acrostic, perhaps showing exhaustion."},
{q:"How does Lamentations help us lament as Christians?",prep:"It names pain to God, admits sin, and doesn't rush to resolution. Compare the lament Psalms and Jesus weeping over Jerusalem (Luke 19:41). Romans 12:15."}]},
{b:"Lamentations",s:3,e:5,title:"His mercies are new every morning",
ctx:"Chapter 3, the center of the book, voices one suffering man ('I am the man who has seen affliction'). In the middle of the darkness he says: 'The steadfast love of the LORD never ceases; His mercies never come to an end; they are new every morning' (3:22-23). Chapter 4 compares before and after the siege, and chapter 5 is a communal prayer ending with an honest question: 'unless You have utterly rejected us.'",
themes:["Hope in the middle of the darkness","Hesed","Waiting quietly","Restore us"],
qs:[{q:"Explain the turning point in Lamentations 3:19-24.",prep:"'This I call to mind': hope is a choice to remember God's character. The LORD is my portion. Hesed (steadfast love) and faithfulness. Hope is in God, not in circumstances."},
{q:"What does the unresolved ending (5:21-22) teach?",prep:"Faith can live with unanswered questions. Plead 'Restore us to Yourself.' Jews read 5:21 again after 5:22 to end on hope. Our hope is fulfilled in Christ."}]},
// ================= EZEKIEL =================
{b:"Ezekiel",s:1,e:3,title:"The glory of the LORD by the Chebar",
ctx:"593 BC, the 5th year of Jehoiachin's exile. Ezekiel, a priest aged 30 deported in 597 BC, sees God's glory by a canal in Babylon: living creatures, wheels full of eyes, and a sapphire throne. The point: God is not limited to the Jerusalem temple. He is with the exiles. Ezekiel eats a scroll and is appointed a watchman for Israel.",
themes:["God's mobile glory","Commissioned to a rebellious house","Eating God's word","The watchman"],
qs:[{q:"What is the significance of Ezekiel seeing God's glory in Babylon?",prep:"Exiles thought God was confined to the land or defeated. The throne-chariot has wheels that move in every direction. God is sovereign everywhere. Compare Revelation 4."},
{q:"Explain the watchman principle (3:16-21) and its relevance to ministry.",prep:"Responsibility to warn, while the hearer is responsible for responding. Blood on the watchman's hands if he is silent. Paul in Acts 20:26-27. Balance faithfulness with the results belonging to God."}]},
{b:"Ezekiel",s:4,e:7,title:"Siege dramas: the end has come",
ctx:"Ezekiel acts out Jerusalem's coming siege: a clay-brick model, lying on his side 390 days and then 40, eating rationed bread, and shaving his hair and dividing it into thirds (4–5). Many exiles still believed Jerusalem was safe. Chapters 6–7 announce: 'An end has come upon the four corners of the land.'",
themes:["Acted prophecy","Certain judgment","Idolatry on the mountains","Then they will know that I am the LORD"],
qs:[{q:"Why did God use such dramatic sign-acts with Ezekiel?",prep:"Hard-hearted listeners. Ezekiel had been made mute except when God opened his mouth (3:26-27). Visual preaching. The cost to the prophet, who embodies the message."},
{q:"Trace the phrase 'then you will know that I am the LORD' in Ezekiel.",prep:"It appears around 70 times. The purpose of both judgment and salvation is the knowledge of God. God acts for His name's sake."}]},
{b:"Ezekiel",s:8,e:11,title:"The glory departs the temple",
ctx:"592 BC. In a vision Ezekiel is carried to Jerusalem and shown four abominations in the temple: the idol of jealousy, elders worshiping images in secret, women weeping for Tammuz, and men worshiping the sun with their backs to the temple (8). A man in linen marks those who grieve over sin (9). Then the unthinkable happens: God's glory leaves the temple step by step and departs to the Mount of Olives (10–11). A new heart is promised (11:19).",
themes:["Hidden idolatry","Sealing the faithful","Ichabod: the glory departs","A new heart"],
qs:[{q:"Trace the stages of the glory's departure (9:3; 10:4, 18-19; 11:22-23).",prep:"From the cherub to the threshold, to the east gate, to the mountain east of the city (the Mount of Olives). It returns in 43:1-5. Jesus ascends from the Mount of Olives and will return there (Acts 1:11-12; Zechariah 14:4)."},
{q:"What does the 'mark on the forehead' in Ezekiel 9 teach?",prep:"God distinguishes those who sigh and groan over sin. The mark (Hebrew 'taw') protects. Compare Revelation 7:3 and 9:4, the Passover blood. Judgment begins at the sanctuary (1 Peter 4:17)."}]},
{b:"Ezekiel",s:12,e:15,title:"False prophets and a useless vine",
ctx:"Ezekiel packs an exile's bag and digs through a wall, a picture of Zedekiah's escape attempt and capture (12; fulfilled in 2 Kings 25:4-7). False prophets whitewash a crumbling wall (13). Elders have set up idols in their hearts (14). Even Noah, Daniel and Job could only save themselves. Judah is a useless vine, good only for fire (15).",
themes:["Precise fulfilment","Whitewashed walls","Idols of the heart","Fruitless vine"],
qs:[{q:"What are 'idols in the heart' (14:3-7)?",prep:"Inner loyalties that compete with God. They can be present without any statue. Seeking God while clinging to them is rejected. Apply this to modern idols such as approval, comfort and control. Compare 1 John 5:21."},
{q:"How does the vine image (15) connect with John 15?",prep:"A vine has value only through its fruit. Israel failed. Jesus, the true vine, makes abiding possible. Branches that don't abide are burned."}]},
{b:"Ezekiel",s:16,e:19,title:"The unfaithful bride and personal responsibility",
ctx:"Chapter 16 is one of the most graphic allegories in Scripture: Jerusalem as an abandoned baby whom God rescued, married and adorned, who then became a prostitute with other nations' gods. Yet God promises an everlasting covenant (16:60). Chapter 17 is a riddle of eagles (Babylon, Egypt and Zedekiah). Chapter 18 refutes the proverb 'The fathers eat sour grapes and the children's teeth are set on edge': each soul answers for its own sin. Chapter 19 laments Judah's kings.",
themes:["Grace forgotten","Spiritual adultery","Individual responsibility","God takes no pleasure in death"],
qs:[{q:"How do you reconcile Ezekiel 18 ('the son shall not bear the iniquity of the father') with Exodus 20:5?",prep:"Exodus 20:5 refers to those who 'hate Me' and continue in the sin, as patterns and consequences across generations. Ezekiel 18 is about guilt and personal accountability. Repentance is possible: 'Why will you die?' (18:31-32)."},
{q:"Why does God use such shocking imagery in Ezekiel 16?",prep:"To make the people feel the betrayal. Idolatry is adultery against a loving Husband. Grace came first (16:6, 'Live!'). The covenant is restored and they are ashamed, not proud (16:63). Compare Ephesians 5:25-27."}]},
{b:"Ezekiel",s:20,e:24,title:"A history of rebellion and the siege begins",
ctx:"591–588 BC. God recounts Israel's history of rebellion, yet He acts 'for My name's sake' (20). A sword is sharpened (21), and the crown will be removed 'until He comes whose right it is' (21:27). Jerusalem is the 'bloody city' (22), and God seeks a man to stand in the gap but finds none (22:30). Two sisters, Oholah (Samaria) and Oholibah (Jerusalem), appear (23). On the very day the siege begins (January 588 BC) Ezekiel's wife dies, and he is told not to mourn as a sign (24).",
themes:["For My name's sake","The rightful King coming","Standing in the gap","Grief as a sign"],
qs:[{q:"Who is 'He whose right it is' in Ezekiel 21:27?",prep:"Echoes Genesis 49:10 ('until Shiloh comes'). The crown is removed from Zedekiah. A messianic hope fulfilled in Christ, the son of David (Luke 1:32-33)."},
{q:"How should we read the death of Ezekiel's wife (24:15-27)?",prep:"Real, costly grief. 'The delight of your eyes,' an expression of love. Not mourning outwardly signals a loss too great for normal grief, the temple. The prophet's life is given for the message."}]},
{b:"Ezekiel",s:25,e:28,title:"Oracles against nations: Tyre and its king",
ctx:"While Jerusalem is besieged, Ezekiel speaks against Ammon, Moab, Edom and Philistia for gloating (25). Then a long section on Tyre, the rich island trading power: its destruction (26), a lament for it as a magnificent sinking ship (27), and its proud king who says 'I am a god' (28). Ezekiel 28:11-19 describes him in Eden-like terms as a guardian cherub, which many apply to Satan's fall.",
themes:["Gloating judged","Wealth and pride","The fall of the arrogant","Hope for Israel (28:25-26)"],
qs:[{q:"How should Ezekiel 28:11-19 be interpreted?",prep:"The primary meaning is a lament for the king of Tyre. The Eden and cherub language goes beyond a human. Many see Satan as the power behind the king (similar to Isaiah 14). Hold the views humbly; the common lesson is pride and beauty corrupted."},
{q:"Was the prophecy against Tyre (26) fulfilled?",prep:"Nebuchadnezzar besieged it for 13 years (586–573 BC), and 29:18 acknowledges limited plunder. Alexander the Great built a causeway with the rubble of the mainland city in 332 BC ('throw your stones into the water,' 26:12). Prophecy can be fulfilled progressively through 'many nations' (26:3)."}]},
{b:"Ezekiel",s:29,e:32,title:"Oracles against Egypt",
ctx:"Seven oracles against Egypt, mostly dated 587–585 BC (29:17 dates to 571 BC). Pharaoh is a great sea monster who claims 'the Nile is mine; I made it.' Egypt was the 'staff of reed' that Judah leaned on, and it splintered. Assyria, a great cedar that fell, is the example (31). The section ends with a funeral dirge as Egypt descends to the Pit with the other fallen nations (32).",
themes:["False trust in Egypt","The pride of Pharaoh","God brings down superpowers","Descent to the Pit"],
qs:[{q:"Why is Egypt condemned as a 'staff of reed' (29:6-7)?",prep:"It promised help and failed, injuring those who leaned on it. Compare Isaiah 36:6. The lesson about trusting human powers. Psalm 20:7."},
{q:"What does Ezekiel 31 teach by using Assyria as an example?",prep:"Assyria fell despite its greatness. History is a sermon. Pride goes before destruction, and the cedar image appears again in Daniel 4."}]},
{b:"Ezekiel",s:33,e:36,title:"The city has fallen: a new heart",
ctx:"The turning point: in January 585 BC a fugitive arrives saying 'The city has fallen' (33:21), and Ezekiel's mouth is opened. From here his message turns to hope. The watchman charge is renewed. God condemns selfish shepherds and promises to shepherd His sheep Himself through 'My servant David' (34). Mount Seir is judged (35). Most important: God will cleanse His people, give a new heart and a new spirit, and put His Spirit within them (36:25-27).",
themes:["From judgment to hope","The Good Shepherd","A new heart and Spirit","For My holy name"],
qs:[{q:"How does Jesus fulfil Ezekiel 34?",prep:"John 10: 'I am the Good Shepherd.' God Himself shepherds (34:11-16), and 'My servant David' shepherds (34:23-24). Both are true in Jesus, fully God and the Son of David. Luke 15 and 19:10: seeking the lost."},
{q:"Connect Ezekiel 36:25-27 to John 3:5.",prep:"'Born of water and Spirit': Nicodemus, as a teacher of Israel, should have known Ezekiel 36. Cleansing and a new heart: regeneration. This parallels the New Covenant of Jeremiah 31."}]},
{b:"Ezekiel",s:37,e:39,title:"Dry bones and Gog of Magog",
ctx:"The valley of dry bones: a whole army of exiles who said 'our hope is lost' comes to life through the prophetic word and the Spirit's breath (37:1-14). Two sticks become one, so Judah and Israel are reunited under one King, David, with an everlasting covenant of peace and God's sanctuary among them (37:15-28). Then Gog of Magog leads a vast coalition against restored Israel, and God Himself destroys them (38–39).",
themes:["Resurrection life by the Spirit","Reunification","Everlasting covenant","Final victory of God"],
qs:[{q:"What is the primary meaning of the dry bones vision, and how may it be applied?",prep:"Primary: the restoration of the nation (37:11-14). The process is the word, then the breath (ruach, which means breath, wind and Spirit). Applications include personal regeneration, revival, and the final resurrection. Note: 'I will put My Spirit in you.'"},
{q:"Survey views of Gog and Magog (38–39).",prep:"Futurist (literal end-time invasion), symbolic (the final rebellion of evil), and links to Revelation 20:8. The common lesson is that God defends His people and is glorified before the nations. Avoid speculative headline matching."}]},
{b:"Ezekiel",s:40,e:43,title:"A vision of the new temple and the glory returns",
ctx:"April 573 BC, the 25th year of exile. A man with a measuring reed gives Ezekiel a detailed tour of a new temple: gates, courts and chambers. The symmetry speaks of perfect holiness and order. The climax is 43:1-5: the glory of the LORD returns from the east and fills the house. 'This is the place of My throne.'",
themes:["Holiness measured","God's presence restored","Order and worship","Shame leading to obedience"],
qs:[{q:"Survey interpretations of Ezekiel's temple (40–48).",prep:"Literal millennial temple; symbolic picture of restored worship; typological fulfilment in Christ and the church (John 2:19-21; Revelation 21–22). Strengths and weaknesses of each. All agree it shows God dwelling with His people."},
{q:"Why is 43:1-5 the climax of the whole book?",prep:"The glory left (chapters 10–11) and now returns. Ezekiel's ministry is framed by the glory (chapter 1). Jesus: 'we beheld His glory' (John 1:14). God's goal is to be present with His people."}]},
{b:"Ezekiel",s:44,e:48,title:"The river of life and 'The LORD Is There'",
ctx:"Regulations for priests (the faithful sons of Zadok), the prince and offerings (44–46). Then a river flows from under the temple threshold, getting deeper, until it heals the Dead Sea, with trees whose leaves are for healing (47). The land is redistributed among the 12 tribes, and foreigners receive an inheritance too (47:22). The city's new name is 'Yahweh Shammah,' THE LORD IS THERE (48:35).",
themes:["Life flowing from God's presence","Healing","Inheritance for all","God with us"],
qs:[{q:"Connect the river of Ezekiel 47 to John 7:38 and Revelation 22.",prep:"Water from the temple. Jesus: 'rivers of living water' (the Spirit). Revelation 22:1-2: the river from the throne and leaves for the healing of the nations. Life gets deeper the further it goes."},
{q:"Why is 'The LORD is there' a fitting end to Ezekiel?",prep:"The book's theme is God's presence: lost because of sin, restored by grace. Compare Immanuel and Revelation 21:3. The goal of salvation is God Himself."}]},
// ================= DANIEL =================
{b:"Daniel",s:1,e:3,title:"Faithful in Babylon",
ctx:"605 BC. Nebuchadnezzar deports Judah's elite youths, including Daniel, Hananiah, Mishael and Azariah, renamed with Babylonian names to reshape their identity. Daniel 'resolved not to defile himself' with the king's food. Nebuchadnezzar dreams of a statue of gold, silver, bronze and iron mixed with clay, smashed by a stone that becomes a mountain: God's kingdom (2). Daniel's three friends refuse to bow to the golden image and are thrown into the furnace, and a fourth figure walks with them (3).",
themes:["Faithful but not isolated","God reveals mysteries","Four kingdoms then God's kingdom","Even if He does not"],
qs:[{q:"Interpret the statue of Daniel 2. What are the four kingdoms?",prep:"The common conservative view: gold = Babylon (stated in 2:38), silver = Medo-Persia, bronze = Greece, iron = Rome. The stone cut 'without hands' is God's kingdom in Christ, which grows to fill the earth. Note 2:44. Compare with Daniel 7."},
{q:"What does Daniel 3:16-18 teach about faith?",prep:"'Our God is able... but even if He does not.' Faith trusts God's power and His will, and does not demand an outcome. God is present in the fire (Isaiah 43:2). Apply it to pressure to conform today."}]},
{b:"Daniel",s:4,e:6,title:"Kings humbled and the lions' den",
ctx:"Nebuchadnezzar boasts over Babylon and is driven to live like an animal until he acknowledges 'the Most High rules the kingdom of men' (4). In 539 BC Belshazzar uses the temple vessels at a drunken feast, a hand writes MENE, MENE, TEKEL, PARSIN, and that night Babylon falls to the Medes and Persians (5). Under Darius, jealous officials trap Daniel over his prayer life. He prays anyway, and God shuts the lions' mouths (6).",
themes:["God humbles the proud","Weighed and found wanting","Consistent prayer","God's kingdom endures"],
qs:[{q:"Compare Nebuchadnezzar's response (4) with Belshazzar's (5).",prep:"Both were proud. Nebuchadnezzar was humbled and praised God; Belshazzar knew all this (5:22) yet defied God. Knowledge brings responsibility. Grace was given to one, judgment fell on the other the same night."},
{q:"What can we learn from Daniel's prayer habit (6:10)?",prep:"Three times a day, toward Jerusalem (1 Kings 8:46-49), 'as he had done previously.' Discipline built before the crisis. Integrity: they could find no fault except his faith. Compare 1 Peter 3:15-16."}]},
{b:"Daniel",s:7,e:9,title:"Beasts, the Son of Man and seventy weeks",
ctx:"The book's second half: visions. Chapter 7 (c. 553 BC): four beasts rise from the sea, and the Ancient of Days takes His throne. 'One like a son of man' comes with the clouds and receives an everlasting kingdom, and Jesus' favourite self-title comes from here. Chapter 8: a ram (Medo-Persia) and a goat (Greece, Alexander), and a little horn (Antiochus IV) who defiles the temple. Chapter 9: Daniel reads Jeremiah's 70 years, confesses the nation's sin, and Gabriel brings the 'seventy weeks' prophecy pointing to the Anointed One being 'cut off.'",
themes:["The Son of Man","God rules history","Confession prayer","Messiah cut off"],
qs:[{q:"Why is Daniel 7:13-14 central to understanding Jesus?",prep:"'Son of Man' coming with clouds receives dominion and worship. At His trial Jesus quotes it (Mark 14:62) and is charged with blasphemy, because He claimed divine authority. It combines a human figure with divine prerogatives."},
{q:"Outline Daniel's prayer in 9:3-19 as a model of intercession.",prep:"Grounded in Scripture (reading Jeremiah). Fasting, sackcloth. He confesses 'we have sinned' and identifies with the people. He appeals to God's character and mercy, 'not for our righteousness,' and for His name's sake. The answer comes while he is still praying."}]},
{b:"Daniel",s:10,e:12,title:"Kings of the north and south and the resurrection",
ctx:"536 BC, the 3rd year of Cyrus. After 21 days of fasting, Daniel sees a glorious man. An angel explains he was delayed by the 'prince of Persia' (spiritual warfare, 10:13). Chapter 11 gives detailed history of Persian kings, Alexander, and the wars between the Ptolemies (south) and Seleucids (north), culminating in Antiochus IV Epiphanes (175–164 BC), who foreshadows a final antichrist figure. Chapter 12 has the clearest OT statement of resurrection: some to everlasting life, some to shame (12:2).",
themes:["Spiritual warfare","God's control of detailed history","Persevere under persecution","Resurrection and reward"],
qs:[{q:"What does Daniel 10 reveal about the spiritual realm and prayer?",prep:"Heard from 'the first day.' Angelic conflict with the 'princes' of nations, and Michael's role. Compare Ephesians 6:12. Avoid fear and speculation: the point is that God's purposes prevail, so we should persist."},
{q:"How does Daniel 12:1-3 shape Christian hope?",prep:"A time of trouble, deliverance for those written in the book, a bodily resurrection to two destinies, and the wise shining like stars. Jesus echoes it in John 5:28-29 and Matthew 13:43. Faithfulness until the end (12:13)."}]}
];
